    <string name="german_why_subject">Subject of the clause, so it is in the nominative.</string>
    <string name="german_why_direct">Direct object of the verb, so it is in the accusative.</string>
    <string name="german_why_dative">Dative object: %1$s takes the dative.</string>
    <string name="german_why_dative_plain">Dative object (indirect object), so it is in the dative.</string>
    <string name="german_why_after_prep">It follows the preposition \"%1$s\", which takes the %2$s.</string>
    <string name="german_why_reflexive_verb">Reflexive: it repeats the subject, with the verb %1$s.</string>
    <string name="german_why_reflexive">Reflexive: it repeats the subject of the clause.</string>
    <string name="german_why_relative">Refers back to \"%1$s\" and starts a clause with the verb at the end. Gender and number come from that noun, the case from its job inside the clause.</string>
    <string name="german_why_relative_plain">Starts a relative clause with the verb at the end. Gender and number come from the noun it refers to.</string>
    <string name="german_why_demonstrative">Probably a demonstrative pronoun standing on its own, not an article before a noun.</string>
    <string name="german_why_question_main">Question word: the verb comes second.</string>
    <string name="german_why_question_sub">Question word in an indirect question: the verb goes to the end.</string>
    <string name="german_why_determiner">Goes with \"%1$s\" and shows its case, gender and number.</string>
    <string name="german_why_after_determiner">Adjectives after it take %1$s endings.</string>
    <string name="german_why_prep_case">\"%1$s\" takes the %2$s.</string>
    <string name="german_why_prep_twoway">Two-way preposition (Wechselpräposition): accusative for movement to a place, dative for position. Here: %1$s.</string>
    <string name="german_why_prep_fixed">Fixed with \"%1$s\": here it does not work as a two-way preposition.</string>
    <string name="german_why_prep_contraction">Contraction of the preposition and the article \"%1$s\".</string>
    <string name="german_why_coord">Joins words or clauses and does not change the verb position.</string>
    <string name="german_why_subord">Starts a subordinate clause: the verb goes to the end (%1$s).</string>
    <string name="german_why_adj_attr">Stands before \"%1$s\" and agrees with it, so it takes an ending: -%2$s (%3$s declension).</string>
    <string name="german_why_adj_pred">Predicate adjective after \"%1$s\": it has no ending.</string>
    <string name="german_why_adj_adv">Adjective used as an adverb with \"%1$s\": it has no ending and is not a separate adverb.</string>
    <string name="german_why_adv_verb">Modifies the verb \"%1$s\".</string>
    <string name="german_why_adv_sentence">Comments on the whole sentence.</string>
    <string name="german_why_adv_front">It opens the clause, so the verb comes second and the subject follows it.</string>
    <string name="german_why_comparable">It has comparative forms.</string>
    <string name="german_why_not_comparable">It cannot be compared.</string>
    <string name="german_why_degree">Degree word: it strengthens \"%1$s\".</string>
    <string name="german_why_tentative">Not certain: the line leaves more than one reading open.</string>
